WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.376 --> 00:00:03.478
(playful music)

00:00:03.478 --> 00:00:05.391
- [Voiceover] Was our
first feature Point Break?

00:00:05.391 --> 00:00:06.525
Did we do that before Tremors?

00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:08.103
- [Voiceover] No, not officially yet.

00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:08.936
- [Voiceover] Right.

00:00:08.936 --> 00:00:10.593
So as a company, Tremors would be the first,

00:00:10.593 --> 00:00:11.893
technically the first feature.

00:00:11.893 --> 00:00:12.785
- Yeah, I guess.

00:00:12.785 --> 00:00:13.769
I don't know.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:15.590
Someone should Google that.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.257
(playful music)

00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:24.042
(clears throat)

00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:24.875
- [Voiceover] Are you
gonna ask us questions,

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:26.215
or do you want us to start talking?

00:00:26.215 --> 00:00:27.590
- [Voiceover] Could you
introduce each other?

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:28.779
- I would love to.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:30.896
- I would like to introduce
you to Tom Woodruff, Jr.,

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:33.868
who's the co-founder of
Amalgamated Dynamics.

00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:35.596
- I'd like to introduce
you to Alec Gillis.

00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:37.231
He's the co-founder of ADI,

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:40.921
home of the Oscar-winning
creature effects team.

00:00:40.921 --> 00:00:42.853
- [Voiceover] When people
think of practical effects,

00:00:42.853 --> 00:00:46.290
creatures, you think of
non-computer generated characters.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.203
We've been lucky to be involved
with some of the biggest

00:00:48.203 --> 00:00:50.896
and best of those types of monsters.

00:00:50.896 --> 00:00:52.345
We did the worms in Tremors --

00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:53.460
- [Voiceover] We did the fat makeups

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.853
on Tim Allen in the Santa Clause --

00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:56.841
- [Voiceover] All the
practical animals in Jumanji --

00:00:56.841 --> 00:00:58.048
- [Voiceover] We've done the Alien,

00:00:58.048 --> 00:00:58.995
we've done the Predator --

00:00:58.995 --> 00:01:00.704
- [Voiceover] Starship Troopers.

00:01:00.704 --> 00:01:01.782
- I wasn't done.

00:01:01.782 --> 00:01:03.082
- Well, you can keep goin', buddy.

00:01:03.082 --> 00:01:06.612
- Of course, there's always the gorilla.

00:01:06.612 --> 00:01:07.770
Cut, right, now, that's perfect.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.961
Now we cut to putting the gorilla costume.

00:01:09.961 --> 00:01:12.628
(hip hop music)

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:14.903
We started performing our own creatures,

00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:17.559
because we both had
this sort of observation

00:01:17.559 --> 00:01:20.828
of stunt men, despite their
talents and their skills.

00:01:20.828 --> 00:01:23.002
Them going into a creature
suit, and not really knowing

00:01:23.002 --> 00:01:25.367
how to make a creature suit work.

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.318
- It would be very weird
for us to just say,

00:01:27.318 --> 00:01:28.804
we're gonna build
something, and then bring

00:01:28.804 --> 00:01:31.404
it to set, and say, "Now what?"

00:01:31.404 --> 00:01:32.654
Are you hungry?

00:01:33.603 --> 00:01:34.436
You want some food?

00:01:34.436 --> 00:01:35.330
Are you hungry?

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.045
If you're a digital animator,
you build the character,

00:01:37.045 --> 00:01:39.256
and you follow through,
and you make it move,

00:01:39.256 --> 00:01:41.157
and you bring it to life,
so that's our philosophy.

00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:44.983
The final step is performance,
'cause that's where it lives.

00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:46.692
You want to try a little tiny piece?

00:01:46.692 --> 00:01:47.525
Here.

00:01:47.525 --> 00:01:48.358
Go ahead.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:49.191
Go ahead.

00:01:50.408 --> 00:01:51.241
What do you think?

00:01:51.241 --> 00:01:52.117
You want some more.

00:01:52.117 --> 00:01:53.083
You, hey, hey!

00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:55.312
He's, listen, listen!

00:01:55.312 --> 00:01:57.559
I can get you fresh bread.

00:01:57.559 --> 00:01:59.726
I can get you fresh bread.

00:02:01.318 --> 00:02:02.730
Tremors is an example of a film

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.386
that was from the pre-digital era.

00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:07.374
If you look at it, the practical effects,

00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:10.402
they've stood the test of
time, and we're proud of that.

00:02:10.402 --> 00:02:12.891
- Digital effects can do things
that we can't possibly do.

00:02:12.891 --> 00:02:15.306
Practical effects have a presence to them,

00:02:15.306 --> 00:02:18.371
that even the best CGI work gets close to,

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.693
but doesn't completely achieve.

00:02:20.693 --> 00:02:22.736
In other words, there's
more emotion, I believe,

00:02:22.736 --> 00:02:25.634
to a practical creature,
than to a digital creature.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:29.498
- Wave to the camera, and say good-bye.

00:02:29.498 --> 00:02:30.854
Bye bye.

00:02:30.854 --> 00:02:31.687
Bye.

00:02:35.182 --> 00:02:36.015
(sighs)

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:37.932
He's so temperamental.

